# stick-figure fix log

## epoch 0 (2026-11-20): created from a reference reel
Made by Claude from references/stick-figure.mp4: a 37.4 s, 1080 x 1920, 30 fps narrated reel rendered from the /stick-figure skill's built-in example in github.com/artemnovitckii/claude-animation-skills (MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Artem Novitckii): a code-animated "what if / why" stick-figure explainer with a Kokoro TTS voice (am_michael at speed 1.12), a synthesised plucked underscore and code sound effects. Analysis: reference-lab/runs/stick-figure (contact sheet, crops, survey and review answered by Claude), committed copy in reference-lab/refs/stick-figure. The engine's own source (SKILL.md, web/shots.js, env.js, chars.js, lib.js, main.js, script.json, audio.py) was read as well, and is more precise than the video.

What was taken from the video (rea-video + Claude's eyes):
- 12 setups for 10 lines (the tool splits it into 16 at in-shot light changes and impact flashes), 24 cuts a minute, mean shot 2.3 s; whip pans of about 0.2 s between places, hard cuts inside a sequence and into black.
- Narrative line: hook question on the character who holds the answer -> "picture this" (the viewer as "you") -> the others flee -> you follow and the problem lands -> the mechanism with one real number on a diagram -> slapstick consequence -> the turn (the insider's trick) -> why it works (seen through his eyes) -> a real test, stamped -> a payoff line that flips the hook.
- Rendering: no pixel grid; flat fills 79%, gentle gradients 5%; dark outlines on about a third of hard edges; 38 colours cover 95%; detail score 6.2; no held frames (animated on ones).
- Colour scenes: bright warm-ground / cool-sky day (complementary), near-black darkness (L 0.12), a hot yellow glow turning into navy for the diagram, warm amber interior at the end. Thin accents measured with oklch_colours.py.
- Sound (video/sound.md): voice 78% of the time, male (about 124 Hz), about 3 words a second; music measured 139.7 BPM (the source declares a 132 BPM 6/8 jig), sections change at the dark part, the reveal and the payoff; 15 audible effect hits (pops, a click, a ding) in 37 s, most on visual hits and whips.
- Captions: 1 or 2 lowercase words, white with a dark outline, popping in at about 70% of the height.

What was taken from the source (exact techniques):
- Character: the stick rig (bean torso, round white head with a cel shade, brows, eyes with pupils and highlights, mouths, hats, 2-bone IK arms, contact shadow) is already the engine's h.stick (ported from chars.js); the template makes every character asset call it and maps the pose sheet's names to its named poses, with reachL/reachR for holding and touching, silhouette + eyesGlow for the dark beat, squash for landings.
- Backgrounds layered as env.js does: sky gradient, additive sun glow with turning rays, outlined clouds drifting, far landmarks in 3 tones hazed toward the sky by distance, a water band with sliding dashes and a bright horizon line, a back wall with posts and a cap rail, a perspective plank floor (lines to a vanishing point above the frame, every third plank lighter, seam ticks); the interior's boards, posts, beam and perspective floor lit by an additive light shaft or lamp; the diagram's full-frame radial glow with rays, crossing to a cold gradient with twinkling dots.
- Camera from shots.js: per-shot push-in, handheld = two summed sines (3 to 4 px), punch-in 1.0 -> 1.75 on the trick, pull-out 1.6 -> 1.0 at the end; impacts = jagged burst with a sound word, 30 px shake decaying in 0.25 s with a small roll, a warm 35% flash, orbiting stars; landings = squash + dust puffs; the darkness reveal as a growing radial-gradient hole with an iris ring; the proof as a ticked checklist card and a slammed rubber stamp with knocked-out ink; the number counting up beside a clock whose wedge fills.
- Palette from the source's hex values, rewritten as OKLCH relations by role (ink L 0.24 violet-grey; shirts as primaries C 0.14 to 0.21; warm wood under a cool sky; darkness, glow and accent ramps).
- Script shape from script.json, topic stripped into placeholder line shapes; music feel from audio.py (plucked pizzicato and glockenspiel jig, minor in the dark part, bright major from the proof).
Not copied: the topic and its objects, places, lines, numbers and the named TV show (the template uses placeholders only).

Engine limits met (not changed; engine files belong to the main session):
- Captions: `captions: words` gives 1 or 2 lowercase words like the reference, but at y 1560 and 96 px (the reference: y about 1345, 70 px); no keyword colour. The template keeps faces and in-scene text out of y 1480 to 1640.
- Transitions: the engine adds its own short horizontal smear entering every shot and cannot do the reference's whip (a 420 px slide with heavy motion blur, 0.2 s out and 0.2 s in) or choose cut versus whip per shot. The template fakes the outgoing half with a 180 px camera slide and speed streaks in the last 0.2 s of each shot.
- Camera: the engine adds a neutral 3% zoom drift on top of each shot's own camera; kit.bg has no camera or parallax input, so day scenes lose the source's depth layers (sky 0.08 to floor 1.0); backgrounds slide their own layers with lt instead.
- One line per shot: the reference has 12 setups for 10 lines; the template allows one cut inside a shot (picture beat) to get closer to its cut rate.
- Fonts: Poppins (captions, numbers) and Lora (the card) are not available; Segoe UI bold and Georgia stand in.
- Music and effects come from the engine's library fit and its 7 effect kinds (pop, whoosh, boom, knock, splash, thunder, zap), not the source's synthesised jig and cartoon sounds (boing, clang, tweet, scribble, riser, tick).
- Narrator: the reference's Kokoro am_michael maps to eleven-v4 roger, which has no measured speaking rate yet, so the first reel's length may drift.

epoch 1: onion-sf-e1 ("why onions make you cry", 42.5 s, 9 shots, MiniMax $0.271, reel total $0.351 with voice; grade: rendering 0.06, rhythm 0.22, colour 0.69 (the topic's green and orange against the reference's sea blue))
- Right: h.stick characters read well and keep their look (striped hero, cap, faces: dizzy, scared, sad); word captions (`captions: words`) work like the reference; bright cartoon place with layered trees and fence; the glow stage with the mechanism asset and a 30 seconds clock; the proof card with ticks and a stamp. Voice roger 5.34 st PASS, music fit PASS, voice over music 12 dB.
- Flaw: the beats copied the reference's plot instead of the topic: neighbours "panic and flee the kitchen" at a knife, a black scene with only glowing eyes for an onion, an "insider" wearing an onion over the mouth, a "gardener" doing the trick -> fix: beats rewritten to follow the topic (hook, picture, problem, mechanism 1-2, effect, turn, proof only with a real test, payoff); the insider is now a "helper" who would know the trick, extras and darkness only when the topic has them.
- Flaw: a false hook ("What if cutting an onion had nothing to do with your eyes?") and empty lines ("The documented mechanism explains why chilling slows the reaction.", "So onions don't attack you") -> fix: the hook must be true as asked; every line says the actual thing, never announces it; the payoff answers the hook plainly.
- Flaw: a wooden sign with no words in the payoff -> fix: never a sign, board, card or label without its words.
- Flaw: shot 8 (proof card only) rejected 6 times "largest subject 0 px" -> fix: the hero stands beside the proof card at 450 to 600 px. Many acting rejections at 3 s beats -> acting beat 4; 7 poses rejected twice -> fewer beats (8 or 9 shots).
